     1	/*
     2	 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
     3	 * The board-independent part of the FPGA test bench (../README.md): the chip as submitted to
     4	 * Tiny Tapeout (tt_um_chip_top), a player that plays the host's side of the host link from a
     5	 * stored list of frames, and a recorder that logs every change of the chip's pads with its clock
     6	 * number. A processor reads and writes it through a small register bus; fpga_axil.v puts that bus
     7	 * on AXI4-Lite.
     8	 *
     9	 * Everything, the bus included, runs on the chip's one clock (60 MHz on the chip). Once the
    10	 * processor has loaded the frames and set start, the run is fixed clock by clock: the processor's
    11	 * timing does not reach the chip, so a run on the board and a simulation of this file with the
    12	 * same frames should record the same trace.
    13	 *
    14	 * Frames (written by ../chip-top/bin/fpga_ops.exe), one 32-bit word each:
    15	 *   [15:0]  clocks to hold these levels, 1..65535; 0 ends the run
    16	 *   [19:16] D, the level the host drives on the host link data lines uio[3:0]
    17	 *   [20]    the host drives D (otherwise the lines are left to the chip)
    18	 *   [21]    S, the host link strobe (uio[4])    [22] R, the read request (uio[5])
    19	 *   [23]    sample the data lines at the start of the frame's first clock (the levels of the
    20	 *           clock before, as ../chip-top/bin/edge_phase.ml's host sees them)
    21	 *   [31:24] ui_in
    22	 *
    23	 * Register map (byte offsets; reads have side effects only where noted):
    24	 *   0x00 R   identifier 0x46423031 ("FB01")
    25	 *   0x04 W   bit 0: start (reset the chip for RESET_CLOCKS, then play from frame 0);
    26	 *            bit 1: stop (back to idle, chip held in reset)
    27	 *        R   bit 0 busy, bit 1 done, bit 2 trace full (entries were dropped),
    28	 *            bit 3 ran off the end of the frame memory without an end frame
    29	 *   0x08 RW  RESET_CLOCKS, clocks of reset before the first frame (default 16, at least 2)
    30	 *   0x0C RW  UI_MASK: bits of ui_in taken from the ui_ext pins as well (ORed with the frames')
    31	 *   0x10 RW  FRAME_ADDR, where the next FRAME_DATA write goes
    32	 *   0x14 W   FRAME_DATA: store a frame at FRAME_ADDR and advance it (ignored while busy)
    33	 *   0x18 R   clocks played since the first frame
    34	 *   0x1C R   free-running clock counter (read it twice, some time apart, to measure the clock)
    35	 *   0x20 R   samples taken
    36	 *   0x24 RW  SAMPLE_ADDR
    37	 *   0x28 R   the sample at SAMPLE_ADDR (bits 3:0); the read advances SAMPLE_ADDR
    38	 *   0x2C R   trace entries recorded (bits 30:0); bit 31 set if the trace filled up
    39	 *   0x30 RW  TRACE_ADDR
    40	 *   0x34 R   entry TRACE_ADDR, word 0: the clock number
    41	 *   0x38 R   word 1: {uio_out 2nd half, uio_out 1st half, uo_out 2nd half, uo_out 1st half}
    42	 *   0x3C R   word 2: {7'b0, rst_n, uio_in, ui_in, uio_oe}; the read advances TRACE_ADDR
    43	 *
    44	 * Trace: one entry for the first clock played and one for every clock in which any recorded
    45	 * level differs from the clock before. The chip's output stage changes pads on both clock edges,
    46	 * so each output is recorded twice per clock: as it was just before the falling edge (first
    47	 * half) and just before the next rising edge (second half). Inputs are recorded as the chip is
    48	 * given them during the clock.
    49	 */
