## Findings

1. **High — Vivado build stops at cfgmem generation.** [`build.tcl:87`](/home/matthias/prog/janestreet/emulator/prototypes/fpga-kv260/vivado/build.tcl:87) calls `write_cfgmem` without the required `-size` argument. After implementation succeeds, Vivado will reject this command, so the script will not produce the advertised `.bin` or reach XSA export. AMD’s Tcl reference marks `-size` as required for `write_cfgmem`. [AMD UG835](https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/ug835-vivado-tcl-commands/write_cfgmem)

2. **Medium — AXI writes ignore byte strobes, including zero-strobe writes.** [`fpga_axil.v:71-75`](/home/matthias/prog/janestreet/emulator/prototypes/fpga-kv260/rtl/fpga_axil.v:71) saves WDATA but not WSTRB, and [`fpga_axil.v:95`](/home/matthias/prog/janestreet/emulator/prototypes/fpga-kv260/rtl/fpga_axil.v:95) marks WSTRB unused. Consequently, a legal write to offset `0x04` with `WSTRB=0000` and `WDATA=1` still starts playback; a partial write to `0x08` also replaces the whole reset-clock value. The AW/W split-cycle handling and read-side-effect pulse did not show a concrete defect in my review.

3. **Medium — the checker can accept a run with a malformed trailing UART start.** [`check.py:51-58`](/home/matthias/prog/janestreet/emulator/prototypes/fpga-kv260/host/check.py:51) stops scanning UART starts during the final ten bit periods. I confirmed with a synthetic trace that a falling edge in that window produces no decoded UART record. If a run has the expected complete bytes and then an extra grid-aligned, truncated start near the end, the byte check ignores it and the edge-spacing check can still pass.

4. **Low — host-drain decoding discards untagged sample pairs.** [`check.py:97-100`](/home/matthias/prog/janestreet/emulator/prototypes/fpga-kv260/host/check.py:97) retains only pairs whose tag byte has bit 7 set. Extra or malformed samples with an untagged byte are silently ignored; [`check.py:136-138`](/home/matthias/prog/janestreet/emulator/prototypes/fpga-kv260/host/check.py:136) does not compare the sample count to the metadata. Such extra records can therefore coexist with a passing host-drain check.

## Checks and limits

I rebuilt the bench from the current RTL, ran the supplied demo frames, and ran `check.py`: it completed with 2,131 frames, 10,753 clocks, and all demo decodes passing. That checks the normal demo path, not the malformed-trace cases above.

The full `run_sim.sh` could not start: its chip-regeneration step uses a hard-coded `/var/tmp` `mktemp` path, which is read-only in this environment. I did not find a concrete frame-prefetch, reset/restart, end-frame, or recorder half-clock defect in the RTL review. I also could not confirm Vivado’s handling of the inverted chip clock or its final timing constraints without a Vivado run; those remain unverified.
